# Normalize line endings to LF for all text; let Git detect text vs binary.
#
# This alone is not enough: a file written with CRLF by a tool after checkout
# stays CRLF in the working copy, and anything measuring file content then gets
# a different answer locally than on a fresh LF checkout. That is how the skill
# front-matter budget in src/__tests__/mcp-payload.test.ts first failed on CI
# while passing here. That test now normalizes before it measures.
* text=auto eol=lf
bun.lock -diff linguist-generated=true
*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.glb binary
*.webp binary
*.tgz binary
*.ico binary

# Retained programs and runtime inputs are identified by their exact bytes.
# Checkout conversion would invalidate source, skill and build receipts.
*.kiln.js -text
src/** -text
dist/** -text
scripts/create-workspace.mjs -text
package.json -text
plugin.json -text
.claude-plugin/** -text
docs/**/*.mjs -text

# These two Troy sources are byte-sealed inputs of the pinned archer pose bank.
# Preserve their original bytes; text checkout conversion invalidates that bank.
scenes/packages/troy/web/archer-bank.mjs -text
scenes/packages/troy/web/archer-bow.mjs -text

# `render-service/src/**` was in the list above and is deliberately not any more.
# Its bytes ARE hashed -- `fingerprintRendererInputs` walks that directory into the
# renderer's capture identity -- but `-text` only froze whatever was committed, and
# what was committed was inconsistent: four files fully CRLF, `renderer.mjs` at
# 395 of 399 lines, three carrying a single stray CRLF line, four fully LF. The
# `* text=auto eol=lf` rule at the top gives what the fingerprint actually needs,
# which is ONE line ending on every platform, and it makes the directory editable
# by ordinary tooling. Provenance receipts record the identity a poster was made
# under; a later renderer build hashing differently is the point of recording it.
